TESTING STEPS: (ROUGH DRAFT OUTLINE)

This testing outline is adapted from a testing assignment we had in another of the classes.


#1 - COMPILATION/INTERPRETER

This phase isnt necessarily part of the use case list or functionality testing, but it is designed to make sure 
we are all on the same page and testing the same stuff.

I'll obviously need all the files with some basic intstructions and comments on how to set them up and run them.  

If the files are interpreted, e.g. python, please include a venv with all the files and dependencies zipped into
into one complete zip file; or at least provide the list of modules, dependencies, etc on how to setup.

If the files a compiled, or need need to be compiled, please provide complete structure with compilation instructions.

Make sure all data is provided, too.


#2 - TURNING EVERYTHING ON

The first thing I will look for is to see if the receiver can be turned on, and if it is listening okay.

Next, I'll try some basic use cases to ensure the sender is working.

Last, check to see if everything shuts down well enough.

The reason for this is - if it cant perform a simple send and receive transmission, then the more complex use cases 
probably wont work either.


#3 - USE CASE SCENARIOS

Assuming everything is setup, running and looking basically good...

Please provide ALL the use case commands you want me to test, arguments and parameters,
along with some indication of the expected output. 

The point here is to make sure I'm testing the same code, under the same conditions, in anticipation of the same results.

That includes error messages, or even if the code fails completely for some reason.  Meaning - sometimes a programmer might
not be able to figure something out - so if it is an known run time error, logic error, syntax error, etc, it's probably good
to know it has been previously identified.


#4 - OUTPUT 

By output, here I mean any significant expected output, files, etc... the program is supposed to generate.

That would also include even error messages the compiler or interpreter might generate.

The results should show the app performs, delivers and fulfills its purpose. 


#5 - FINAL REPORT

In conclusion, I will write my final testing report, so as to point out everything is working as it should, point out
possible errors, limitations, suggest possible changes or improvements, and so on. 

If helpful or necessary, I may try to include any interpreter or compiler messages, as appropriate.

But, basically, I will primarily look to see if the app fulfills the assignment specs.

